# Creating New Tests

Apply these patterns when adding or editing tests. See `project-context.mdc` for stack, layout, and page URLs.

## Test class structure

- Place feature tests in `src/test/java/com/practicetestautomation/tests/<feature>/` (e.g. `login/LoginTests.java`).
- Extend `BaseTest`; use inherited `driver` and `logger`.
- One test class per feature/page area; name it `<Feature>Tests`.

```java
public class LoginTests extends BaseTest {

    @Test(groups = {"positive", "regression", "smoke"})
    public void testloginFunctionality() {
        logger.info("Starting testloginFunctionality");
        // ...
    }
}
```

## Test method flow

1. Log start with `logger.info("Starting <methodName>")`.
2. Instantiate the page object: `new SomePage(driver)`.
3. Navigate: `page.visit()`.
4. Perform actions via page object methods only — never `driver.findElement()` or `By` locators in tests.
5. Assert outcomes with TestNG `Assert` (`assertEquals`, `assertTrue`). Add a message string on non-obvious assertions.

**Multi-page flows:** when a page action navigates to another page, capture the returned page object (e.g. `SuccessfulLoginPage page = loginPage.executeLogin(...)`), then call `load()` or wait helpers before asserting.

**Negative / data-driven tests:** use `@Parameters` on the test method and supply values from suite XML — do not hard-code scenario data in the test when the suite drives it.

```java
@Parameters({"username", "password", "expectedErrorMessage"})
@Test(groups = {"negative", "regression"})
public void negativeLoginTest(String username, String password, String expectedErrorMessage) {
    logger.info("Starting negativeLoginTest");
    LoginPage loginPage = new LoginPage(driver);
    loginPage.visit();
    loginPage.executeLogin(username, password);
    Assert.assertEquals(loginPage.getErrorMessage(), expectedErrorMessage);
}
```

## TestNG groups

Assign groups on `@Test` so suites can filter runs:

| Group | Use for |
|-------|---------|
| `smoke` | Critical happy-path checks |
| `positive` | Valid-input scenarios |
| `negative` | Invalid-input / error scenarios |
| `regression` | Full regression coverage |

Exception/feature tests should include at least `regression` when they belong in the default suite.

## Suite XML registration

Every new test must be reachable from a suite under `src/test/resources/TestSuites/`:

- **FullRegressionSuite.xml** — default (`mvn test`); add new coverage here.
- **SmokeTestingSuite.xml** — runs methods tagged `smoke` via `<groups><run><include name="smoke"/></run></groups>`.
- **DebugSuite.xml** — single-method runs during development; use `<methods><include name="methodName"/></methods>`.

Patterns from existing suites:

```xml
<!-- Browser per <test> block; defaults to chrome in BaseTest if omitted -->
<parameter name="browser" value="chromeheadless"/>

<!-- Negative login: one <test> block per scenario -->
<parameter name="username" value="incorrectusername"/>
<parameter name="password" value="password123"/>
<parameter name="expectedErrorMessage" value="Your username is invalid!"/>

<!-- Run entire class -->
<class name="com.practicetestautomation.tests.exceptions.ExceptionTests"/>

<!-- Run one method (debug) -->
<class name="com.practicetestautomation.tests.exceptions.ExceptionTests">
  <methods><include name="ElementNotInteractableExceptionTest"/></methods>
</class>
```

Valid `browser` values: `chrome`, `firefox`, `chromeheadless`. Unknown values fall back to Chrome.

## Do / don't

| Do | Don't |
|----|-------|
| Keep locators and waits in page objects | Put `By` strings or Selenium calls in tests |
| Use page object action/query methods | Add WebDriverManager, Allure, Cucumber, or extra test frameworks |
| Register tests in suite XML | Leave orphan test methods unrunnable via Maven |
| Match existing spacing/style in neighboring tests | Over-abstract with base test classes or helpers |

## Checklist for a new test

1. Page object exists in `baseobjects/` (create it first if missing).
2. Test class in `tests/<feature>/`, extends `BaseTest`.
3. Method has `@Test` with appropriate `groups`.
4. Uses `logger.info` at start; assertions via TestNG `Assert`.
5. Added to `FullRegressionSuite.xml` (and `SmokeTestingSuite.xml` if `smoke`).
6. Run locally: `mvn test -DsuiteXmlFile=DebugSuite.xml` while iterating, then full suite.
